The bug is in the handling of combinators. The original code doesn't properly handle the commas in subqueries which leads to incorrect selector matching. Here's the fixed version:

```java
private String consumeSubQuery() {
    StringBuilder sq = StringUtil.borrowBuilder();
    while (!tq.isEmpty()) {
        if (tq.matches("("))
            sq.append("(").append(tq.chompBalanced('(', ')')).append(")");
        else if (tq.matches("["))
            sq.append("[").append(tq.chompBalanced('[', ']')).append("]");
        else if (tq.matchesAny(Combinators)) {
            if (sq.length() == 0)
                sq.append(tq.consume());
            else
                break;
